{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/defect_cocycle_with_findings.tex","new_string":"\\begin{remark}[A solver defect surfaced by Part~IV, since fixed]\\label{rem:Esolver}\nThe \\emph{killed-height} operator (Exp.~E) at first told a different story: its\npower iteration did not converge (relative residuals $3$--$9\\%$; all sectors hit\nthe $3000$-iteration cap), because the height-transport mode produces a\nnear-degenerate band of top eigenvalues, and several reported gap ratios\n\\emph{exceeded $1$}---impossible, since with $\\lvert\\Lcal_\\chi\\rvert$ equal to the\nprincipal operator on the same support Wielandt's theorem forces\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)\\le\\rho(\\Lcal_0)$. The solver was replaced by a \\textbf{restarted\ncomplex Arnoldi} (Krylov subspace, Hessenberg shifted-QR Ritz values, validated\nagainst known spectra in the unit tests). The killed-height operator now converges\nto residual $<8\\times10^{-11}$ with no Wielandt violations.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Target 1 (the finiteness leak), on the converged solver}\nWith Arnoldi, the killed-height gap\n$\\kappa_Q=1-\\min_\\chi[\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)/\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\chi_0})]$ (minimum over the\nfirst four multiplicative characters) is \\emph{tilt-independent} (the earlier\n$s$-dependence was non-convergence) and \\emph{grows} with $Q$:\n\\[\n\\kappa_9=0.093,\\quad \\kappa_{27}=0.190,\\quad \\kappa_{81}=0.306,\\quad\n\\kappa_{243}=0.329 .\n\\]\nOver the tested range the gap is bounded well away from $0$ and increasing---the\ndirection the uniform-gap hypothesis $\\kappa_Q\\gtrsim 1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$\nrequires. (Caveat: minimum over four characters, not all $\\phi-1$; the\nfiniteness leak as $Q\\to\\infty$ is not settled.)\n\\end{findingbox}","old_string":"\\begin{remark}[A solver defect surfaced by Part~IV]\\label{rem:Esolver}\nThe \\emph{killed-height} operator (Exp.~E) is a different story. Its power\niteration did not converge (relative residuals $3$--$9\\%$; all sectors hit the\n$3000$-iteration cap), because the height-transport mode produces a\nnear-degenerate band of top eigenvalues. Several reported gap ratios\n\\emph{exceeded $1$}, which is impossible: with $\\lvert\\Lcal_\\chi\\rvert$ equal to\nthe principal operator on the same support, Wielandt's theorem forces\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)\\le\\rho(\\Lcal_0)$. The killed-height $\\kappa_Q$ values are\ntherefore unreliable and require an Arnoldi / shift-invert solver. The\n\\emph{residue-only} gaps above are the trustworthy object and are the correct one\nfor a residue character sum (the tilt enters $W$ only through the weight\n$2^{s\\Delta}$). Consequently Target~1's quantitative form is read off F, not E;\nthe qualitative conclusion---nontrivial sectors are gapped, weakest at $s=1$\n(Target~3)---holds.\n\\end{remark}","replace_all":false}